          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Does \"with\" require the two terms to sit next to each other in the documentation, or is it an index convention?",
          "answer": "In the context of documentation and indexing, \"with\" often indicates a relationship or association between two terms. It does not necessarily require them to sit next to each other in the documentation.\n\nWhen used in documentation, \"with\" can signify that one term is connected to another, even if there are words or sections in between. In indexing, \"with\" is commonly a convention to link related terms, showing that they are associated, even if they appear far apart in the text.\n\nIn summary, \"with\" suggests a connection rather than immediate proximity, and it can be used both in actual documentation and as an indexing convention to highlight relationships between terms.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
